 "id": "interjection",
 "el": "Το επιφώνημα",
 "title": "The interjection",
 "span": "A1–C1",
 "blurb": "Αχ, ωχ, μπράβο, ουφ, έλα: the sounds and words of surprise, pain, approval, annoyance and greeting, and the exclamatory sentence.",
 "intro": [
  {
   "p": "Interjections (**επιφωνήματα**) express a feeling or a reaction in a single word: surprise, joy, pain, disgust, approval, relief. They stand outside the grammar of the sentence, do not decline, and are usually followed by an exclamation mark. Traditional Greek grammar lists them as the tenth part of speech."
  },
  {
   "p": "Many are simple sounds (**αχ, ωχ, ουφ**); others are words borrowed from other parts of speech (**μπράβο, κρίμα, έλα, Θεέ μου**). Using them well is a sign of fluency: they are among the first things Greeks notice in a foreigner's speech."
  }
 ]
